  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Newcomers Navigating the Welfare State is already showing early scholarly and practical reach. The book has 3 citations in OpenAlex, 1,213 recorded downloads, and an open-access instance in repository records, indicating that it is being read and taken up while its citation profile is still developing. Its topic also aligns strongly with policy concerns around reduced inequalities and migrant access to welfare services.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2023, Newcomers Navigating the Welfare State addresses a timely question: how migrant newcomers encounter and negotiate welfare institutions. The subject matter sits at the intersection of bureaucracy, welfare state policy, immigration, and social work, making the book relevant not only to scholarship but also to practitioners and policymakers working on access to social assistance in increasingly diverse societies.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest uptake signal in the record is usage. The book has attracted 1,213 downloads through OAPEN, suggesting sustained reader interest rather than a purely nominal presence in the scholarly ecosystem. Those downloads are a meaningful sign of consultation, especially for a policy-facing book whose arguments may be used by researchers, students, and professionals interested in welfare administration and newcomer inclusion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence is still early but visible: OpenAlex currently records 3 citations. The citing works span migration and integration research, including studies published in Citizenship Studies and the British Journal of Social Psychology, as well as a preprint in SSRN Electronic Journal. This pattern suggests that the book is beginning to travel beyond its immediate field into adjacent conversations about citizenship, migration, solidarity, and lived experience.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s social relevance is also reinforced by its alignment with the Sustainable Development Goal on reduced inequalities. Together with the funding support recorded from KU Leuven and the Belgian Federal Science Policy Office, the record shows a work that was developed within institutional contexts attentive to public value. Taken as a whole, the evidence points to a book with clear policy relevance, growing scholarly visibility, and substantial early readership, even though its longer-term citation impact is still emerging.</p>",
